.TH START_FRESH 1 2026-11-01 dotfiles "User Commands"
.SH NAME
start_fresh \- set up a fresh Fedora KDE Plasma install
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B start_fresh
.RB [ \-c ]
.RB [ \-p ]
.RB [ \-h ]
.PP
.B curl \-fsSL https://raw.githubusercontent.com/dmalyuta/dotfiles/fedora/start_fresh | bash
.RB [ "\-s \-\-"
.IR options ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B start_fresh
sets up a Fedora KDE Plasma machine the way its author uses it: software repositories, packages
and apps, the NVIDIA driver, these dotfiles, low-level hardware settings and the KDE desktop.
.PP
It is safe to re-run: every step skips what is already done. Re-running it also updates what it installed from outside the package
manager, like OpenRGB and PdfCraft, and the Flatpak apps.
.PP
It asks all its questions up front, along with the sudo password, so the rest runs unattended.
The answers are remembered as the defaults for the next run.
.PP
On a new machine, run it through
.B curl
as shown above. It then clones the dotfiles repository to
.IR ~/Projects/dotfiles .
Run from a checkout of the repository, it uses that checkout instead.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-c
Reuse the answers from the last run instead of asking the setup questions again. A question that
was never answered is still asked.
.TP
.B \-p
Plain output: print everything as is, without the progress display.
.TP
.B \-h
Show a short help message and exit.
.SH WHAT IT DOES
In order:
.IP 1. 4
Asks the setup questions: whether to upgrade to a newer Fedora release, if there is one (yes by
default, and asked every run while one is available, even with
.BR \-c ),
which optional apps to install (Superluminal, the Brother printer driver,
PDF-XChange Editor), whether to restore a CoolerControl backup, the custom OBS configuration on the
desktop, the sleep debug log on the laptop, and the VS Code workspaces for the Meta+letter hotkeys.
With Secure Boot on, it also asks for a one-time password for enrolling the kernel module signing
key.
.IP 2. 4
Adds the software repositories: RPM Fusion, Terra, COPRs, and the vendors' own for Brave, VS Code,
NordVPN and Grafana.
.IP 3. 4
Sets up kernel module signing, with Secure Boot on.
.IP 4. 4
Upgrades the installed packages, installs the missing ones, OpenRGB from upstream on the desktop,
and builds the kernel modules (NVIDIA, OpenRazer) for the newest kernel.
.IP 5. 4
Installs and updates the Flatpak apps, and installs apps from upstream to
.IR ~/.local :
Oh My Posh, Bazelisk, the SN Pro font, PdfCraft, RAD Debugger, and Superluminal and PureRef if
chosen.
.IP 6. 4
Sets up the SSH key, clones the dotfiles if needed, and links them into
.I ~
and
.IR ~/.config .
.IP 7. 4
Configures the apps and services: Git, OpenRGB, OBS, Grafana, keyd, Syncthing (also at
.IR http://syncthing.localhost ),
Anaconda, Wine, Flameshot and the system journal.
.IP 8. 4
Runs
.I scripts/configure_hardware
(GPUs, kernel, USB wake sources, sleep and hibernation, the ASUS laptop),
.I scripts/sleep\-debug/setup
on the laptop, and
.I scripts/configure_kde
(input devices, hotkeys, shortcuts, virtual desktops, effects, theme, panels).
.IP 9. 4
If the Fedora upgrade was chosen, downloads it with
.BR "dnf system\-upgrade download" .
The reboot at the end installs it. Without it, the packages are still upgraded within the current
release in step 4.
.PP
At the end, if a change needs a reboot to apply, it lists what needs it and asks: ENTER reboots,
ESC finishes without rebooting. With the Fedora upgrade downloaded, ENTER reboots with
.BR "dnf offline reboot" ,
which installs it; after ESC, run that later to install it.
.SH PROGRESS DISPLAY
In a terminal, and without
.BR \-p ,
it shows its progress in place below the terminal's earlier output: a spinner and a bar of how far
through its steps it is, the current step, and a box with the tail of its output. Questions and
password prompts appear in the box, and are answered there as usual.
.PP
The display is
.IR scripts/tui.py ,
which needs the
.B python3\-pyte
package; the first run installs it. When the script ends, the last view stays in the terminal, and
the full output is saved to
.IR ~/.cache/start_fresh.log .
.PP
The display runs the script in a terminal of its own, where sudo would ask for the password
again. So
.B start_fresh
has sudo remember the password across all of the user's terminals, see
.I /etc/sudoers.d/timestamp\-global
below: then it doesn't ask if the password was entered in any terminal in the last 5 minutes.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/Projects/dotfiles
The dotfiles repository, cloned there on a new machine.
.TP
.I ~/Projects/dotfiles/.start_fresh_answers
The answers from the last run (git-ignored).
.TP
.I ~/.cache/start_fresh.log
The full output of the last run under the progress display.
.TP
.I ~/Downloads
Where it works, and where it looks for the installers that need a manual download (PureRef,
PDF-XChange Editor).
.TP
.I /etc/sudoers.d/timestamp\-global
Has sudo remember the password across all of the user's terminals, for 5 minutes. Delete it to go
back to sudo's default of one terminal at a time.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Done.
.TP
.B 1
A bad option, or installing the packages failed.
.TP
.B 130
Stopped with Ctrl+C.
.SH EXAMPLES
Set up a new machine:
.PP
.RS
.B curl \-fsSL https://raw.githubusercontent.com/dmalyuta/dotfiles/fedora/start_fresh | bash
.RE
.PP
Re-run from the checkout with the last answers, to apply updates and changes to the dotfiles:
.PP
.RS
.B ~/Projects/dotfiles/start_fresh \-c
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR sudoers (5),
.BR dnf (8),
.BR flatpak (1)
.SH AUTHOR
Danylo Malyuta.
